## Decisions

### Should I make the recurring laptop recipe a repository script, with setup and recovery instructions alongside it?

The existing laptop maintenance commands are:

```sh
go run ./tools/import
go run ./tools/fetchsignin
go run ./tools/pdfthumbnails
```

`import` already runs the Veracross and school-website exporters, updates people and groups, imports missing portraits and classroom update decks, and synchronizes the school calendar. `fetchsignin` starts the capture browser and fetches documents using its Google sign-in. `pdfthumbnails` renders missing PDF pages locally with Poppler and uploads them for server thumbnails. All three write to production; import can also incur Claude charges. I have only inspected files, not run these commands.

- (*) Repository script plus instructions
  I would put the ordered commands in one script, document prerequisites and recovery in `docs/dev.md` (the developer guide), and point to it from `AGENTS.md` (the agent instructions). For the three existing commands above, you would run one command such as `sh tools/catchup.sh`; it would run import, then signed-in fetching, then PDF rendering, stopping at the first failed command. This makes the executable recipe versioned with the tools and usable outside byre. A byre launcher could call that same script later.
- ( ) Written checklist only
  I would put the three commands above, in that order, in a recurring-maintenance section of `docs/dev.md`, with prerequisites and recovery instructions, and point agents there. You would run each command yourself and inspect its output before proceeding. This gives you a pause between production steps but leaves you responsible for following the sequence.

ian@fc.run chose: Repository script plus instructions
